 <int>3</int>
 <string>
  
    
  
  
    &lt;h2&gt;
      Exercises to accompany Tutorial 6
    &lt;/h2&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      5/5/15
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;h3&gt;
      Exercise 1 (of 3)
    &lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      Each of the derivations in the following Exercise can be done using the 
      Rule of Elimination of the Conditional ⊃E, and the rules learned 
      previously.
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      If a formula in a line of a derivation has the form
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p style=&quot;margin-left: 40px&quot;&gt;
      &amp;lt;formula1&amp;gt;
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      and a formula in a line of a derivation has the form
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p style=&quot;margin-left: 40px&quot;&gt;
      &amp;lt;formula1&amp;gt; ⊃ &amp;lt;formula2&amp;gt;
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      the Rule of Elimination of the Conditional ⊃E allows you to add
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p style=&quot;margin-left: 40px&quot;&gt;
      &amp;lt;formula2&amp;gt;
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      The elimination of ⊃ {⊃E} is the classical inference rule known as Modus 
      Ponens. In English, if you have A and If A then B you are entitled to B.
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      Show each of the following arguments to be valid.
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (a) F, F⊃G ∴ G
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (b) F⊃G, F ∴ G&amp;amp;F
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (c) F⊃G, G⊃H,F ∴H
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (d) F⊃(G⊃H), F⊃G,F ∴H&amp;amp;F
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (e) F⊃((~G)⊃H), F⊃~G,F ∴H&amp;amp;F
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;h3&gt;
      Exercise 2 (of 3)
    &lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      Each of the derivations in the following Exercise can be done using only 
      the rule the Rule of Elimination of the Biconditional ≡E, and the rules 
      learned previously.
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      If the formula in a line in a derivation has the form
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p style=&quot;margin-left: 40px&quot;&gt;
      &amp;lt;formula1&amp;gt;≡&amp;lt;formula2&amp;gt;
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      the Rule of Elimination of the Biconditional ≡E allows you to remove the 
      biconditional and add either
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p style=&quot;margin-left: 40px&quot;&gt;
      &amp;lt;formula1&amp;gt;⊃&amp;lt;formula2&amp;gt;
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      or
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p style=&quot;margin-left: 40px&quot;&gt;
      &amp;lt;formula2&amp;gt;⊃&amp;lt;formula1&amp;gt;
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      as an extra line. You have a choice here (on the direction of the 
      conditional).
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      Show each of the following arguments to be valid.
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (a) F≡G ∴ G⊃F&lt;br&gt;(b) F≡G ∴ F⊃G&lt;br&gt;(c) 
      F≡G, G ∴ H∨F&lt;br&gt;(d) F≡G, G≡H, F ∴ H
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;h3&gt;
      Exercise 3 (of 3).
    &lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      After you have started the proof switch on &apos;Tactics&apos; under the Proof &lt;em&gt;Wizard&lt;/em&gt; 
      menu, and do the derivations in the following way. If any of the lines &lt;i&gt;you 
      wish to obtain&lt;/i&gt; has a connective in it, click the appropriate 
      Introduction rule (in these examples this will split the problem in 
      two). If any of the lines &lt;i&gt;you have obtained&lt;/i&gt; has a connective in 
      it, use an Elimination rule to take it out.
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (a) F≡G, H&amp;amp;G ∴G&amp;amp;F
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (b) (F&amp;amp;H)&amp;amp;(R&amp;amp;G) ∴F&amp;amp;G
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;hr&gt;
  

</string>
